==============================================================================
[1] Global r-coordinate identities and the sign convention
==============================================================================
  P_r(r) = 27*a**2*c - 18*a*r + 3*b*r**2 - 2*r**3
  D_r(r) = 3*a - b*r + r**2
  P_r'(r) = -6 D_r(r)  [PASS; the sign is negative]
  on every fibre: P_r(r)=0 and x D_r(r)=r  [PASS]
  disc_r(P_r) = -2916*a**2*(27*a**2*c**2 - 18*a*b*c + 16*a + b**3*c - b**2)
  Tr(r)=3b/2 and Tr(y)=3b/2, whereas Tr(x)=0 (depressed x-core).
  Thus a=0 is a coordinate-collision component independent of L=0.

==============================================================================
[2] Global constant-lead z-cubic: z is integral (PROVED)
==============================================================================
  Q_z(W) = 8 W^3 + A_z W^2 + 6 L B_z W + L C_z
  A_z = 324*a**2*c**2 - 216*a*b*c + 408*a - 15*b**3*c + 6*b**2
  B_z = 27*a**2*c**2 - 18*a*b*c + 52*a + b**3*c + 14*b**2
  C_z = 729*a**4*c**4 - 972*a**3*b*c**3 + 2322*a**3*c**2 + 54*a**2*b**3*c**3 + 270*a**2*b**2*c**2 - 3735*a**2*b*c - 338*a**2 - 36*a*b**4*c**2 + 122*a*b**3*c + 1372*a*b**2 + b**6*c**2 - 2*b**5*c - 80*b**4
  Q_z(F(x,y,z); z) = 0 identically  [PASS]
  lead(Q_z)=8 is constant, hence z is integral over QQ[a,b,c].
  A_z - 12L = 9(b^2 p - 2q)  [PASS]
  Therefore on L=0: Q_z(W)=W^2(8W+A_z).

==============================================================================
[3] Exact L=0 factorization in r and the escaping double shadow
==============================================================================
  r0 = 3*a*(-9*a*c + b)/(-12*a + b**2)
  r1 = (108*a**2*c - 48*a*b + 3*b**3)/(-24*a + 2*b**2)
  modulo L, with q != 0: P_r=-2(r-r0)^2(r-r1)  [PASS]
  q^2 D_r(r0)=27a^2 L, so D_r(r0)=0 on L  [PASS]
  For a != 0, x D_r=r excludes r0 from the affine fibre:
  the double r-root is the shadow of two x-sheets at infinity.

==============================================================================
[4] The finite survivor is a global rational section modulo L
==============================================================================
  (x_s,y_s,z_s) =
    (2*c/(-3*b*c + 4), b - 3*c*(-12*a + b**2)/(-6*b*c + 8), -27*a - 9*b**2*(-3*b*c + 4)/8 + 9*b**2/4)
  p q (r_s-r1)=6bL and p^2(D_r(r_s)-3q/4)=12L  [PASS]
  global identities:
    (3bc-4)^3(F1(x_s,y_s,z_s)-a)=4(54ac^2-27bc+28)L
    (3bc-4)^2(F2(x_s,y_s,z_s)-b)=-36cL
    F3(x_s,y_s,z_s)-c=0
  Hence this is an affine fibre point at every L=0, p!=0 target.
  On L, z_s=-A_z/8; the double W=0 roots are the two escape shadows.

==============================================================================
[5] Exceptional-locus audit
==============================================================================
  p=0: L=q^2/(3b^2), so L=p=0 is
    (a,b,c)=(4/(27t^2),4/(3t),t), t!=0.
    The x-core is the nonzero constant -2c: the fibre is empty.
  q=0: L=b^2 p^2/48, hence the L-slice splits into
    b=0 (the z-axis, one point (c/2,0,0)) or p=0 (empty).
    P_r is triple on both pieces; a triple coordinate root is not
    a three-point affine fibre.
  a=0: P_r=r^2(3b-2r), while L=b^2(bc-1).
    Off L, r=0 is two finite points with the same y=b.
    On bc=1, b!=0, those two sheets escape and the sole point is
    (2/b,-b/2,9b^2/8).
  A_z=0 on L (so Q_z=8W^3) consists of the z-axis, the omitted
    curve, and two curves 9(bc)^2+12bc-28=0 (b p != 0).
    On the last two curves one finite point has z_s=0 and the other
    two W=0 roots are escape shadows: again, not three fibre points.
  generic control (2/27,1,1):
    survivor (2,5/6,-7/8);
    P_r=-2(r-2/3)^2(r-1/6), Q_z=W^2(8W+7).  [PASS]
  c=0 boundary control: a=b^2/16 gives (0,b,-63b^2/16). [PASS]

==============================================================================
[6] Why the double (r,z) root is at infinity
==============================================================================
  The x-core is E=Lx^3+px-2c.  At generic L=0,p!=0 two roots
  satisfy x ~ +/-sqrt(-p/L), whereas y stays bounded by P_r.
  From F3=c, z=2/x^2-3y/x-c/x^3 -> 0.
  Thus (r,z)->(r0,0) records two compactified sheets, not two
  finite affine points.  Only (r1,-A_z/8) is the survivor.

==============================================================================
[7] Pointwise parity rigidity (logical consequence, unchanged)
==============================================================================
  For a real etale polynomial map, conjugation pairs every nonreal
  point of a full complex fibre.  Hence #real = complex degree (mod 2).
  An even-degree wild map cannot have an odd-real-count FULL fibre;
  HYP-9030 test (ii) remains released as stated.

